.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-2-Clause
.Dd August 15, 2026
.Dt IF_PAIR 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm if_pair
.Nd pair of cross-connected point-to-point layer-3 interfaces
.Sh SYNOPSIS
To load the driver as a module at boot time, place the following line in
.Xr loader.conf 5 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
if_pair_load="YES"
.Ed
.Pp
Alternatively, to load the driver at runtime:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
kldload if_pair
.Ed
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
driver provides pairs of point-to-point interfaces that are
cross-connected: a packet transmitted on one interface is received by
its peer.
Like
.Xr epair 4 ,
one side of a pair can be moved into a
.Xr VNET 9
jail to connect it to the host or to another jail.
Unlike
.Xr epair 4 ,
no Ethernet layer is emulated: the interfaces are
.Dv IFF_POINTOPOINT
and carry bare IPv4 and IPv6 packets, so there are no link-layer
headers, no ARP or NDP neighbor resolution and none of the associated
overhead.
Traffic between the two sides is delivered into the peer's protocol
input path by a pool of kernel worker threads, one pinned to each CPU
and shared by all pairs.
Packets are distributed over the workers by a flow hash covering the
source and destination address, the IP protocol and, where applicable,
the TCP or UDP ports, so concurrent flows spread across CPUs while
packets of any one flow are delivered in order.
.Pp
.Xr altq 4
is not supported:
.Nm
delivers through its internal per-CPU queues and has no interface
transmit queue for
.Xr altq 4
to manage.
Traffic between the two sides of a pair can instead be shaped with
.Xr dummynet 4 ,
which operates independently of driver transmit queues.
.Pp
Pairs are created with
.Xr ifconfig 8 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
ifconfig pair create
.Ed
.Pp
This creates the two interfaces
.Dq Li pair0a
and
.Dq Li pair0b .
Destroying either side destroys both halves of the pair.
.Pp
Addresses are configured as on any point-to-point interface: each
side is given its own address and the address of its peer as the
destination.
No shared prefix is needed; a
.Li /32
.Pq or Li /128
host mask is fine.
For example:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
ifconfig pair0a inet 192.0.2.1/32 192.0.2.2
ifconfig pair0b vnet myjail
jexec myjail ifconfig pair0b inet 192.0.2.2/32 192.0.2.1
.Ed
.Pp
The default MTU is 16384.
.Pp
Both sides of a pair are members of the interface group
.Dq Li pair ,
so packet filter rules can match all pairs at once, for example
.Dq Li on pair
in
.Xr pf.conf 5 .
.Pp
The interfaces report link state: up while the pair exists and is
running, down as the first step of its destruction.
.Pp
Transmit checksum offloading
.Pq Cm txcsum , txcsum6
is enabled by default: TCP and UDP checksums of traffic that stays
within the machine are never computed at all, and for traffic
forwarded onward they are computed once at the true egress.
The offloads can be disabled per side with
.Xr ifconfig 8 .
.Sh MTU CONFIGURATION
The large default MTU reduces per-packet overhead for traffic between
the two ends of a pair, which never leaves the machine.
Values between 72 and 65535 are accepted; both sides of a pair should
be configured to the same MTU.
.Pp
Traffic routed onward through a link with a smaller MTU, such as a
physical Ethernet interface, is subject to the usual mechanisms:
TCP connections are sized by the MSS exchange, packets exceeding the
egress link are fragmented or rejected with an ICMP error as required
of routers by RFC 1812 (sections 4.2.2.7 and 5.2.7.1), and path MTU
discovery between a jail and the host is dependable: both ends of a
pair share one machine, so any middlebox interfering with the ICMP
messages it relies on was put there by the local administrator, who
will find its operator unusually easy to reach.
.Pp
To bound the packet size of forwarded traffic while retaining the
large MTU between the two ends of a pair, set an MTU on the routes
that lead beyond the host instead of on the interface, using the
.Fl mtu
route modifier documented in
.Xr route 8 .
For example, a jail whose default route crosses a
.Nm
interface can clamp all forwarded traffic to 1500 bytes:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
route change default -mtu 1500
route change -inet6 default -mtu 1500
.Ed
.Pp
Traffic to directly connected destinations, including the other end of
the pair, continues to use the interface MTU.
Alternatively, lowering the interface MTU on both sides bounds all
traffic, including that between the two ends of the pair.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Connect a new vnet jail to the host and give it a default route
through the pair:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
kldload if_pair
ifconfig pair create
jail -c name=demo vnet persist
ifconfig pair0b vnet demo
ifconfig pair0a inet 192.0.2.1/32 192.0.2.2
jexec demo ifconfig pair0b inet 192.0.2.2/32 192.0.2.1
jexec demo route add default 192.0.2.1
.Ed
.Pp
Verify connectivity in both directions:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
ping -c 3 192.0.2.2
jexec demo ping -c 3 192.0.2.1
.Ed
.Pp
For the jail to reach beyond the host, enable forwarding
.Pq Va net.inet.ip.forwarding
and add address translation or routes for the jail's addresses on the
host as appropriate.
Tear everything down again:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
ifconfig pair0a destroy
jail -r demo
.Ed
.Sh SYSCTL VARIABLES
The
.Nm
driver exposes the following
.Xr sysctl 8
variable, which can also be set as a
.Xr loader.conf 5
tunable:
.Bl -tag -width "net.link.pair.batch"
.It Va net.link.pair.batch
Number of packets a delivery worker processes before briefly
yielding its CPU so that timers and user processes keep running
under sustained load.
The default is 64.
Values above the per-queue limit of 4096 are reduced to it with a
console warning; a value of 0 or less disables yielding, in which
case a saturated worker monopolizes its CPU.
.El
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
Interface counters, shown by
.Xr netstat 1
with the
.Fl id
flags, have the following driver-specific meanings:
.Bl -tag -width "Oqdrops"
.It Ierrs
Received packets that were not parseable as IPv4 or IPv6, for example
malformed packets injected through
.Xr bpf 4 .
.It Oerrs
Packets dropped on transmit because the other side of the pair was
down.
.It Oqdrops
Packets dropped because the receiving side's queue was full, i.e. the
peer's worker could not keep up with the offered load.
.El
.Pp
.Xr tcpdump 1
works on both sides of a pair and shows bare IP packets.
It will report TCP and UDP checksums as incorrect whenever transmit
checksum offloading is enabled; this is the normal appearance of
offloaded checksums and not an error.
.Pp
The delivery workers are visible as kernel threads named
.Dq Li pair_task_ Ns Ar N
in
.Xr top 1 .
A single flow is served by a single worker by design; one busy worker
next to idle CPUs is the expected picture for a single high-volume
connection.
.Pp
If traffic forwarded from a pair undergoes address or port rewriting
on the host and is emitted with invalid checksums, the cause is
typically the in-kernel NAT incompatibility described in the
.Sx BUGS
section.
.Sh SEE ALSO
